{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact R3 head d7cad5d3f745268a4dbbe1d2f88f996a6e375bfc on builder/authority-turn-order-r3-20261109, tree 4afae880, all 26 artifacts, for request 03e5f6d7 under promise 8787bceb and review request 0a801c15. Submit now reads standing right after the signature and shape checks. A definite denial of an ordinary grant refuses before provenance, receiver policy, foreign facts or a turn. Only the alarm drains timed work, and a remote authority run admits at most 4,096 signing keys.\n\nWhat I checked in the source:\n\n- The early refusal. `submit` reads standing once for a key that is not already accepted, before the fresh-admission, receiver and foreign-fact reads. It then rereads `accepted`, so a key accepted during the await keeps its receipt or mismatch path. It refuses `unauthorized` only when the act's `grant` is a string, the read gave a standing, and `heldBy` returns an empty list. A null or unavailable read refuses nothing early. Native grant marks are not strings, so their recovery-key and action-null fallback stays in the commit. The commit calls `held` again on its own head and reading. The early call has no write: it clears `offered`, which the commit's call sets again, and it discards a kept read only when that read is unavailable or the clock is behind, and the commit's call would discard it then too. Only `sealed` records a use. ports.ts now documents that rule.\n- The turn. `Turns.run` drains only when no input waits, that is, when the alarm calls `run(null)`. A waiting input whose clock is behind with something due still ends `clock-behind` without writing. Otherwise the commit sees the due transition and the input ends `busy` with no write. Alarm budgets, ordering and every timed entry's meaning are unchanged.\n- The key bound. `observing` keeps one `keys` set per run. A new key at 4,096 gets `limit` before `config.membership` is read, and existing keys keep their reads. Core maps `limit` to the existing `limit` refusal for acts and preparations, judged at the current head, with no write. Own-Membership standing uses no remote map.\n- The CLI wording changes \"waits for the next act or alarm\" to \"waits for the next alarm\", and the test matches.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main c85a9bae is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 26 paths that its 26 artifacts cover. The commit-message check passes: 16 checked, 0 flagged. Between the repaired gate at 7f560340 and the head, only the note and two manifests change.\n- The seals. I recomputed both whole-file seals outside the builder's worktree. authority.ts gives 7e186fb2… and the new turn.ts seal gives 480fd9b6…, each equal to its literal. The named seal for core.ts `Scope` passes in the gate's Node stage. No route or gate assertion is removed.\n- The gates. I read gate.exit for the original gate at 22b5f36f, tree 8ffde09a: \"exit 1\", Native 1,112 passed, 12 failed and 2 skipped. It stays failed. I read gate.exit for the repaired gate at 7f560340, tree fd8ac86b, which ruling 3da1f865 authorized: \"exit 0\", with Node 144, Counting 56, Plain 4,028 plus one skip and Native 1,124 plus two skips.\n- The tests. Removed lines are the expectations that the old order made true: an act that drains due holds, and \"next act or alarm\". Each replacement runs the real alarm and asserts that the host ran a scheduled alarm. The new order witness checks zero receiver, provenance and foreign reads and no entry on a failed observation. The bound witness fills the real map to 4,096.\n- The evidence. The four manifests hash to the values that the request cites: d7db8261, afe7dbac, cab3cb7b and bd8af79b. Their 93 files match their sizes and digests and are read-only, and the credential scan finds no match.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Kernel section 7: the change adds no kernel package, export, scope kind, record field or reason code. `Authority.read` gains the existing `limit` literal as a runtime port answer. The change moves the runtime's order before recording and leaves every recorded judgment and replay meaning unchanged. Section 7.1's exception for filed security remediation covers it, and no platform service or application guard could make that change instead.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. One residual follows from the bound that request 03e5f6d7 chose, and D6 does not name it. Signing keys cost nothing, and the key enters the set before Membership is read. So any caller who can submit well-formed signed acts to a scope can fill its 4,096 places with throwaway keys. After that, every new key, including a newly joined member's, gets `limit` in that scope until the object starts a new run. Existing keys keep working. I ask the planner to decide whether to accept that residual and name it in D6, or to file a follow-up.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nThe planner deploys all four Workers after landing. No setting changes. This approval authorizes no deployment and closes no live acceptance.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:155e3a622d6462146628af705ab4639221b5bd80","binding":"assigned","head":"d7cad5d3f745268a4dbbe1d2f88f996a6e375bfc","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:003c4f3335c58a7e1e7affde003495ed0eda4d06\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8787bcebc801a3144b562d32b7604f3f69f87836\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:003c4f3335c58a7e1e7affde003495ed0eda4d06","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}